  "result": "Tool calling works like a doctor ordering a lab test.\n\nThe doctor can't run a blood test. They write an order (\"glucose panel, patient 12\"), the lab runs it, and the result comes back. The doctor reads it and decides what to do next.\n\n- **The model is the doctor.** It only writes text, here the order: \"call `check_calendar` for the laser cutter, Friday.\"\n- **Your program is the lab.** It sees the order, runs the real calendar lookup and sends the result back as text.\n- **Tool descriptions are the lab's test menu.** The doctor can only order tests they know exist, and the menu says what each one does.\n\nThe model never touches the calendar itself. It asks, your code acts, and the answer returns as more text for the model to read.\n\nThe analogy breaks in one place. A doctor has years of training on when to order what. The model decides only from the menu descriptions, so vague descriptions lead to wrong orders.\n\nDoes the split between who asks and who acts make sense now?",
